We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)\right\}.
  \end{equation*}
  Given the growth restrictions imposed on the function $f$, with specific consideration given to its dependence on the gradient variable, we find that $p$-differentiability holds when the obstacle is strictly inside the domain, while $q$-differentiability occurs near the boundary of the obstacle, with $p, q \in (1,\infty)$.
